      "name": "raw_long",
      "text": "Large language models generate text one token at a time. At each step the model reads every previous token, computes attention over them, and produces a probability distribution over its vocabulary. Without caching, the cost of each step grows with the length of the sequence, because the keys and values for earlier tokens are recomputed from scratch. A key-value cache stores those tensors after they are first computed, so each new step only processes the newest token. This trades memory for compute, and on a CPU the bottleneck usually becomes memory bandwidth: every generated token requires streaming all of the model's weights from RAM through the cores. Quantization helps precisely because it shrinks the number of bytes that must be moved per token.",
